(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-cms-exam-2-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 2 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which lens corrects myopia, and how?","choices":["No lens; only surgery corrects it","A concave lens, moving the focus back","A toric lens","A prism","A convex lens, which converges light forward"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — negative dioptre. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has blurring of vision in the right eye over two days with pain on eye movement. Colour appears washed out. Examination shows a relative afferent pupillary defect on the right and a normal-looking optic disc. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Amaurosis fugax","Central retinal artery occlusion","Papilledema","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Optic neuritis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Pain on eye movement, dyschromatopsia and a relative afferent pupillary defect, and optic neuritis is among the causes of that defect. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has sudden painless loss of vision in the left eye. Fundoscopy shows a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula. What does the cherry-red spot represent?","choices":["Intact choroid showing through the fovea","Retinal pigment epithelium atrophy","Subretinal fluid at the macula","Haemorrhage into the fovea","A macular hole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The surrounding retina is pale from infarction; the fovea has no inner retinal layers to become oedematous. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has bilateral cataracts and vision of 6/18. He has stopped driving and finds reading difficult. What determines whether he is offered surgery?","choices":["The presence of a relative afferent pupillary defect","His age alone","A fixed visual acuity cut-off","The effect on his daily functioning","His intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the decision is driven by symptoms and function rather than by an acuity threshold alone. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which treatments are named for wet macular degeneration?","choices":["AREDS2 supplementation alone","Scleral buckle","Lens extraction","Anti-VEGF, laser or photodynamic therapy","Laser trabeculoplasty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intravitreal anti-VEGF is first among them. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why is the workup for non-arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy identical to the arteritic form?","choices":["Both require surgery","The workup is not in fact the same","The two have identical treatment","It is a diagnosis of exclusion","Both are treated with steroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — missing arteritis costs the other eye. Giant cell arteritis must be ruled out. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with newly diagnosed open-angle glaucoma is started on treatment. Which class is first line?","choices":["A cholinergic agonist","A topical antibiotic","A prostaglandin analogue","A topical antihistamine","A topical corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prostaglandins or beta blockers lead; cholinergic agents are third or fourth line. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has a suspected open globe injury. Which action must be avoided in any suspected ocular trauma?","choices":["Checking visual acuity","Inspecting the periorbital tissues","Testing confrontation fields","Asking about tetanus status","Dilating the pupil"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. It removes the pupil examination, one of the few windows onto the inside of the eye. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a red eye with a ring of injected vessels radiating from the limbus around the cornea. What does this finding rule OUT?","choices":["Anterior uveitis","Keratitis","Simple conjunctivitis","Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Corneal ulcer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ciliary flush indicates intraocular inflammation and moves the diagnosis to keratitis, uveitis or glaucoma. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man reports heaviness of both upper lids and says he is 'looking through my lashes'. Examination shows excess folds of upper lid skin bilaterally with no inflammation. What examination finding most determines whether surgery will be covered?","choices":["Tear film break-up time","Corneal staining with fluorescein","Intraocular pressure","The measured thickness of the skin fold","A demonstrated visual field defect"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Blepharoplasty is often covered if a field defect is present. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with suspected optic neuritis is referred for imaging. What is ordered?","choices":["Ocular ultrasound","Plain skull radiograph","Computed tomography of the orbits alone","Carotid Doppler","Magnetic resonance of brain and orbits"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. With and without contrast. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 23"},{"q":"Besides lens extraction, what else should be addressed in steroid-related posterior subcapsular cataract?","choices":["The corneal curvature","The steroid burden","The refractive correction","The intraocular pressure alone","The patient's diet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — review the cause as well as removing the lens. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with dry macular degeneration asks whether anything slows it. What is offered?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections","Laser trabeculoplasty","Antioxidant and zinc supplementation","Cataract surgery"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — supplementation slows progression in intermediate dry disease. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has periorbital bruising around BOTH eyes after a head injury, with no direct blow to either eye. What does this suggest?","choices":["Bilateral orbital floor fractures","Battle sign","Raccoon eyes from a basilar skull fracture","Simple bilateral black eyes from the same blow","Subconjunctival haemorrhage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bilateral periorbital bruising without direct trauma. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has a suspected open globe. Which set of signs would support that?","choices":["A mid-dilated fixed pupil and a hazy cornea","Pupil distortion, flat chamber, soft eye","Bilateral disc swelling with normal acuity","Follicles and a tender preauricular node","Greasy lashes with debris at the bases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with extraocular protrusion of uveal tissue and massive haemorrhagic chemosis. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which causes of papilledema are named?","choices":["Tumour, trauma, infection, haemorrhage","Contact lens wear and dry eye","Cataract and macular degeneration","Giant cell arteritis alone","Diabetes, hypertension and hyperlipidaemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin A toxicity as well. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has a basilar skull fracture with clear rhinorrhoea. What does that require?","choices":["Antihistamines","Neurosurgery consultation","Nasal packing","Topical nasal decongestant","Discharge with review in a week"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a cerebrospinal fluid leak mandates it. And admission. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has vertical double vision that is worse when he looks DOWN and tilts his head to one side to compensate. He fell and struck his head last week. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["The fourth cranial nerve","The sixth cranial nerve","The seventh cranial nerve","The third cranial nerve","The second cranial nerve"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superior oblique palsy gives vertical diplopia worse on downgaze, with a compensatory head tilt. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy has orbital cellulitis. What is the usual source of the infection?","choices":["The paranasal sinuses","A corneal ulcer","The lacrimal sac","Haematogenous spread from a distant site","The conjunctiva"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why imaging covers the sinuses as well as the orbits. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has three days of pain, redness and swelling over the OUTER third of her right upper lid, with tearing. There is a tender preauricular node on the same side. Temperature 38.1°C. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pre-septal cellulitis","Dacryocystitis","Hordeolum","Dacryoadenitis","Chalazion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inflammation of the lacrimal gland, which sits under the LATERAL third of the UPPER lid. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has sudden painless loss of the lower half of his visual field in one eye. Fundoscopy shows a wedge of pale retina above the macula. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Central retinal artery occlusion","Branch retinal artery occlusion","Non-arteritic ischaemic optic neuropathy","Branch retinal vein occlusion","Retinal detachment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pale wedge in one artery's territory, producing loss of the opposite field. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man's allergic conjunctivitis has not responded to topical treatment. What does that suggest?","choices":["He should stop all treatment","Surgery is indicated","The diagnosis may be wrong, so refer","He needs oral antibiotics","The dose should simply be doubled"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — symptoms usually settle as allergen levels fall. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has a retinal detachment with the macula still attached. Why does that detail change the urgency?","choices":["It means the detachment will reattach spontaneously","It confirms the cause is traction","Central vision is salvageable before the macula goes","It means surgery can safely be delayed a month","It indicates an exudative rather than rhegmatogenous cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — macula-on detachment is the more urgent repair. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman on long-term oral corticosteroids for rheumatoid arthritis has increasing glare and difficulty reading, worse in bright light. Which cataract type is most likely?","choices":["Traumatic","Congenital","Cortical","Nuclear","Posterior subcapsular"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — associated with steroid use, and it disproportionately affects near vision and glare because it sits on the visual axis. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a central retinal vein occlusion. Which systemic conditions should be sought?","choices":["Asthma and eczema","Inflammatory bowel disease alone","Migraine alone","Osteoarthritis","Hypertension, diabetes, hyperviscosity"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the venous side reflects systemic vascular and rheological disease. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl has mild pre-septal cellulitis. Which organisms does the antibiotic choice need to cover?","choices":["Aspergillus and Candida","Adenovirus","Staphylococcus and Streptococcus","Chlamydia and Neisseria","Pseudomonas and Acinetobacter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — outpatient oral therapy for 10 to 14 days. Including resistant staphylococci. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 53"},{"q":"How is a corneal foreign body removed?","choices":["Topical anaesthetic, then a 27-gauge needle","A cotton bud swept across the cornea","Patching until it works loose","Surgical exploration of the globe","Copious irrigation alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with a battery-operated burr for an iron or copper rust ring. Sterile. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which two skin conditions are associated with blepharitis?","choices":["Psoriasis and lichen planus","Acne vulgaris and folliculitis","Atopic dermatitis and urticaria","Vitiligo and alopecia areata","Rosacea and seborrhoeic dermatitis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, alongside Staphylococcus aureus colonisation. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 19"},{"q":"On slit lamp examination a conjunctival elevation appears pale at its surface and redder at its base. Which two diagnoses does this favour?","choices":["Episcleritis and scleritis","Bacterial and allergic conjunctivitis","Pinguecula and pterygium","Blepharitis and meibomitis","Chlamydial and viral conjunctivitis"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. That is a FOLLICLE. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has swelling of the conjunctiva of the left eye. Which accompanying finding would make this urgent?","choices":["Recent minor eye rubbing","Proptosis with restricted eye movements","Clear discharge from the same eye","A history of hay fever","Mild itching of both eyes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chemosis with proptosis, restricted movement, reduced vision or an afferent pupillary defect means something is filling the orbit. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which demographic typically has an Adie tonic pupil?","choices":["Children under five","Men over seventy","Women in their thirties","Elderly diabetics","Overweight women of childbearing age"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — often unilateral and often asymptomatic. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 6-year-old girl is referred with strabismus. At which levels of the visual motor system can the cause lie?","choices":["The cornea and lens only","The retina alone","The lacrimal system","Brain, nerve, junction or muscle","The optic nerve alone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — causes are found at all four levels. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which mechanisms are named as causing globe rupture?","choices":["A fingernail scratch","Airbag, fist or baseball","Grinding metal","A chemical splash","A fishing hook or knife"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — severe blunt force splitting the eye at a weak point. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has a painful red eye with a 2 mm white corneal infiltrate, an overlying epithelial defect and a small hypopyon. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent referral for scraping and antibiotics","Reassure and review if worse","Topical corticosteroids alone","Topical antibiotic drops four times daily and review in a week","Patch the eye and give oral analgesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A hypopyon indicates significant intraocular inflammation and the eye is at risk. Intensive topical antibiotics after corneal scraping. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 61"},{"q":"What proportion of age-related macular degeneration is the dry form?","choices":["About eighty per cent","About half","About ninety-five per cent","About sixty per cent","About twenty per cent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the wet form accounts for the remaining twenty. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 23-year-old woman has bilateral red, itchy eyes with stringy discharge. Examination shows conjunctival papillae and no preauricular node. Which feature most strongly indicates an allergic cause?","choices":["The bilateral involvement","The absence of a node","The itch","The discharge","The papillae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Itch is the symptom that separates allergic disease from the rest. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man was struck in the eye two days ago. He has lid bruising and swelling but vision is 6/6, the pupils are equal and reactive, and eye movements are full. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Globe rupture","Orbital floor fracture","Orbital and lid contusion","Basilar skull fracture","Hyphema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — soft tissue injury with normal ocular function. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man was elbowed in the eye during football. He has lid swelling and bruising, vision is 6/6, the pupils are equal and reactive, eye movements are full and the globe is intact. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Ice, analgesia and review if symptoms change","Urgent surgical exploration","Immediate computed tomography for all patients","Admit for intravenous antibiotics","Topical corticosteroids"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a simple orbital contusion with normal function needs supportive care. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which treatment is now most commonly used for uveal melanoma?","choices":["Observation alone","Enucleation in all cases","Radiation therapy","Laser trabeculoplasty","Topical chemotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — enucleation is less often needed than it once was. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with an orbital floor fracture is advised not to blow his nose. Why?","choices":["It will displace the fracture fragments","It forces sinus air into the tissues","It will restart the nosebleed","It will cause a cerebrospinal fluid leak","It will worsen the diplopia permanently"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — periorbital subcutaneous emphysema, a recognised feature of orbital floor fracture. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which visual field defect localises the lesion to the optic TRACT?","choices":["A homonymous hemianopia without macular sparing","A monocular field defect","An altitudinal defect","A bitemporal hemianopia","A homonymous hemianopia WITH macular sparing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — post-chiasmal and anterior to the radiations. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man reports gradual painless vision loss over two years. Which three diagnoses should be at the top of the differential?","choices":["Retinal detachment, optic neuritis and angle closure","Conjunctivitis, blepharitis and dry eye","Retinoblastoma, uveal melanoma and iris nevus","Cataract, macular degeneration and open-angle glaucoma","Amblyopia and strabismus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the three commonest causes of gradual painless loss in this age group. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has chronic red, gritty eyes with progressive scarring of the lower palpebral conjunctiva and shortening of the fornix. She also has oral erosions. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Viral conjunctivitis","Trachoma","Allergic conjunctivitis","Mucous membrane pemphigoid","Bacterial conjunctivitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Progressive subconjunctival fibrosis with mucosal involvement elsewhere. The ocular form. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which two mechanisms produce an orbital floor fracture?","choices":["Only a chemical injury","A true blowout, or force buckling the rim","Only a fingernail injury","Only direct penetration of the orbit","Only a high-velocity projectile"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the true blowout is the commoner of the two. Either raised orbital pressure or force on the rim. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with dacryocystitis is febrile, unwell and lives alone with no support. What is the appropriate management?","choices":["Oral antibiotics for 10 days at home","Admit for intravenous, then oral therapy","Topical antibiotics alone","Warm compresses and review in a week","Immediate dacryocystorhinostomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fever, systemic illness or unreliability all move him to inpatient care. Intravenous for 48 to 72 hours, then oral to complete 10 to 14 days. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with advanced open-angle glaucoma is refractory to drops. What follows?","choices":["Temporal artery biopsy","Intravitreal anti-VEGF","Scleral buckle","Peripheral iridotomy","Laser trabeculoplasty"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct, with surgery as the definitive step. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the commonest eye cancer in adults?","choices":["Conjunctival melanoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Iris nevus","Uveal melanoma","Retinoblastoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — from melanocytes of the choroid, ciliary body or iris. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has anterior uveitis. Which finding on slit lamp examination defines it?","choices":["Cells in the anterior chamber","A dendritic lesion","An epithelial defect that stains","A hazy oedematous cornea","Follicles in the fornix"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — inflammatory cells in the aqueous. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 62"},{"q":"An 11-year-old boy with orbital cellulitis is not improving after 48 hours of intravenous antibiotics. Which complication is of greatest concern?","choices":["Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Recurrent chalazion","Pinguecula formation","Refractive change","Chronic dry eye"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intracranial spread gives meningitis or cavernous sinus thrombosis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman reports visual loss in one eye that lasted several hours before clearing. How does the duration affect the diagnosis?","choices":["Duration is irrelevant to the diagnosis","Hours indicates central retinal artery occlusion","Hours indicates optic neuritis","Hours confirms a transient ischaemic attack","Hours argues against a transient ischaemic attack"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the episodes are typically seconds to minutes. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 5"},{"q":"Which conditions are named as causes of chemosis?","choices":["Myopia and astigmatism","Allergy, infection, thyroid and trauma","Cataract and macular degeneration","Retinoblastoma and uveal melanoma","Glaucoma and optic neuritis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, along with orbital cellulitis and impaired orbital venous drainage. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman has bruising over both mastoid processes two days after a fall. What is this sign called, and what does it indicate?","choices":["Raccoon eyes, indicating a basilar skull fracture","Periorbital haematoma from direct trauma","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Battle sign, indicating a basilar skull fracture","Periorbital subcutaneous emphysema"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has transient monocular visual loss attributed to vasospasm. Which drug class is named for that cause?","choices":["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Corticosteroids","Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Antihistamines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the treatment is directed at the underlying cause. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has small, irregular pupils bilaterally. They do NOT react to light, but constrict briskly on near effort. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Horner syndrome","Adie tonic pupil","Third nerve palsy","Physiological anisocoria","Argyll Robertson pupil"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — light-near dissociation with small irregular pupils; 'accommodates but does not react'. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man describes a curtain descending over the vision of his right eye, lasting about five minutes, with complete recovery. He has hypertension and smokes. Examination between episodes is normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer routinely to optometry","Start an oral antihistamine","Reassurance, as vision has recovered","Topical antiglaucoma drops","Carotid imaging and urgent stroke workup"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. It is a transient ischaemic attack of the eye and carries the same stroke risk. — 12. Acute Vision Loss, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which finding after ocular trauma converts a routine review into an emergency referral?","choices":["Lid swelling","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Mild photophobia","A relative afferent pupillary defect","Periorbital bruising"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it indicates optic nerve compromise. — CMS I Ocular Trauma, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 28-year-old healthy woman has bacterial conjunctivitis in one eye, with no contact lens use and no other risk factors. What is the appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical antiviral","Oral antibiotics","Artificial tears alone","A topical antibiotic","Topical corticosteroid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a fluoroquinolone is the named example, with contagious precautions. Broad-spectrum. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has floaters and progressive blurring of vision in one eye over two weeks, with no pain and no redness. Fundus examination shows a focal white chorioretinal lesion with overlying vitreous haze. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis","Posterior uveitis","Episcleritis","Scleritis","Anterior uveitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Choroiditis or retinitis presents with floaters and blurring rather than the pain and redness of anterior disease. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman with headaches and papilledema is found to have a sixth nerve palsy. Why is this described as a false localising sign?","choices":["It is always bilateral","It always indicates a brainstem lesion","It resolves within hours","It only occurs with orbital disease","Its long intracranial course"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct — the palsy does not indicate where the lesion is. Raised pressure anywhere can affect it. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 58-year-old hyperopic woman is warned about one particular ocular emergency. Which is it?","choices":["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Retinal detachment","Optic neuritis","Corneal ulcer","Central retinal vein occlusion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the short eye has a crowded anterior segment. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors, Slide 19"},{"q":"A field defect is found to be MONOCULAR. Where must the lesion be?","choices":["At the chiasm","Posterior to the chiasm","Anterior to the chiasm","In the occipital cortex","In the optic radiation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a monocular defect localises pre-chiasmal. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"Why do BOTH pupils dilate when light is swung onto the affected eye in a relative afferent pupillary defect?","choices":["The efferent pathway on that side is damaged","The perceived illumination has just dropped","The iris sphincter is paralysed","The eye has become dry","The lens has become opaque"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the damaged nerve transmits less, so the brain reads it as less light. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a right homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing after a stroke. Where is the lesion?","choices":["The left occipital cortex","The right optic nerve","The optic chiasm","The left optic nerve","The right occipital cortex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macular sparing is characteristic of an occipital lesion, and the defect is contralateral. — 11. Neuro-Ophthalmology, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has had a lid nodule that has recurred three times in the same spot over eight months, each time treated as a chalazion. Why does the recurrence itself change the plan?","choices":["It indicates the gland is permanently blocked","It means the original diagnosis was a hordeolum","It raises the possibility of sebaceous carcinoma","It suggests underlying blepharitis alone","It indicates an allergic cause"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent lesions, or one persisting beyond two to three months, are referred to rule this out. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which examination findings are described in pre-septal cellulitis?","choices":["Crusting at the lash bases","A discrete non-tender nodule","Diffuse balloon-like lid oedema","A bright red painless patch","Proptosis with painful restricted movement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with variable conjunctival injection, and periocular pain, fever, chills and warmth. With erythema and tenderness. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders, Slide 52"}]};
